IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

-- Goods and Services Tax Act 1993 (Singapore) -- whether GST is charged on what I supply and how (ss 7, 8, 10(2)(a), 21(1), (2),
-- 22(1), Fourth Schedule Part 1), when my supply takes place (s 11(2)), the rate on that day (s 16), how much of my price is GST
-- (s 17(2)), what I may charge when the rate changes after I contract (s 40), and what I pay or get back for a period (s 19(2),
-- (4), (5)).
--
-- SEAT: the individual who makes, or proposes to make, supplies of goods or services in the course of a business in Singapore -- a
-- sole proprietor or a partner -- who may be or become a "taxable person".
--
-- Provisions covered: ss 7, 8(1), (2), (2A)(a), (3); s 10(2)(a); s 11(2); s 16; s 17(2); s 19(2), (3), (4), (5); s 21(1), (2);
-- s 22(1); s 40(1); Fourth Schedule Part 1 paras 1(a), (b), (g), (l), 1A, 1B(a), 2(a), (c).
--
-- Edition: 2020 Revised Edition, informal consolidation, version in force from 11 September 2026 (last amendment S 590/2026), as
-- deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/GSTA1993.txt.
--
-- NOT ENCODED: Seventh Schedule (overseas and marketplace) supplies under s 8(1A); importation (ss 8(4), 18); the Second Schedule
-- (what is treated as a supply -- asked as a fact); the exceptions to the general time of supply (ss 11(3) to 12A); place of supply
-- and belonging (ss 13, 15); the reverse charge (s 14); open market value (s 17(3)); the s 20 rules on which input tax is allowable
-- (asked as a fact); what counts as "international services" under s 21(3) (asked as a fact); ss 21A to 27A reliefs; Part 6
-- special cases; Part 6A transitional splits on a rate change (ss 39A to 39F); the rest of the Fourth Schedule. No regulations
-- under s 41 were deposited, so accounting periods and the input tax allowed by regulation are inputs.
--
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Written straight from the deposited text with the writing-l4-rules skill, from the individual's seat, with
-- the design defaults ruled on 2026-10-18. No pipeline, no coverage table, no independent test pass, no human gate. NOT FOR PUBLIC
-- USE AS ADVICE. See NOTES.md.

§ `Goods and Services Tax Act 1993 -- the charge`

§§ `Fourth Schedule Part 1 -- what I supply`

-- s 22(1): a supply "is an exempt supply if it is of a description or of a class for the time being specified in Part 1 of the
-- Fourth Schedule".
-- Para 1: "(a) the operation of any current, deposit or savings account; (b) the exchange of currency ... other than the supply of a
-- note or a coin as a collector's item, investment article or item of numismatic interest; ... (g) the provision of any loan,
-- advance or credit; ... (l) the provision, or transfer of ownership, of a life insurance contract".
-- Para 1A: "The supply of any investment precious metal specified in Part 2". Para 1B(a): "the exchange of digital payment tokens
-- for currency or other digital payment tokens".
-- Para 2: "(a) any vacant land zoned 'Residential' in the Master Plan ... and used or to be used for residential purposes"; "(c) any
-- land ... with any building on the land ..., being a building which is used or to be used principally for residential purposes".
-- General insurance and commercial property are not in the list.
DECLARE `What I supply` IS ONE OF
    `renting out a flat`                         -- para 2(c): a building used principally for residential purposes
    `selling a flat`                             -- para 2(c)
    `selling vacant land zoned residential`      -- para 2(a)
    `renting out a shop`                         -- not listed: commercial property
    `operating a savings account`                -- para 1(a)
    `exchanging currency`                        -- para 1(b)
    `selling a coin as a collector's item`       -- para 1(b) carves it out
    `lending money`                              -- para 1(g): a loan, advance or credit
    `providing a life insurance contract`        -- para 1(l)
    `providing motor insurance`                  -- not listed: general insurance
    `selling investment-grade gold bars`         -- para 1A, read with Part 2 para 1(a)
    `exchanging digital payment tokens for currency` -- para 1B(a)
    `cutting hair`                               -- not listed
    `selling coffee`                             -- not listed
    `selling goods`                              -- not listed
    `consulting`                                 -- not listed

GIVEN `what I supply` IS A `What I supply`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is what I supply exempt` IF
    CONSIDER `what I supply`
    WHEN `renting out a flat`                             THEN TRUE
    WHEN `selling a flat`                                 THEN TRUE
    WHEN `selling vacant land zoned residential`          THEN TRUE
    WHEN `operating a savings account`                    THEN TRUE
    WHEN `exchanging currency`                            THEN TRUE
    WHEN `lending money`                                  THEN TRUE
    WHEN `providing a life insurance contract`            THEN TRUE
    WHEN `selling investment-grade gold bars`             THEN TRUE
    WHEN `exchanging digital payment tokens for currency` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

§§ `7, 8, 10, 21 -- is GST charged on my supply`

-- s 7: GST "is charged in accordance with the provisions of this Act on the supply of goods and services (including anything treated
-- as such a supply)".
-- s 8(1): "Tax is charged on any supply of goods or services made in Singapore where it is a taxable supply made by a taxable person
-- in the course or furtherance of any business carried on by the taxable person."
-- s 8(2): "A person is a taxable person ... while the person is or is required to be registered under this Act."
-- s 8(2A)(a): a taxable supply is "a supply of goods or services made in Singapore other than an exempt supply".
-- s 8(3): the tax "is a liability of the person making the supply".
-- s 10(2)(a): "supply" ... does not include "anything done otherwise than for a consideration" -- subject to the Second Schedule,
-- which can treat something as a supply (asked as a fact).
-- s 21(1): goods are zero-rated "only if the goods are exported" and services "only if the services are international services";
-- (2) then "no tax is charged on the supply" but "it is in all other respects treated as a taxable supply", "whether or not tax would
-- be chargeable on the supply apart from this section".
DECLARE `My supply` HAS
    `it is made in Singapore`                                     IS A BOOLEAN   -- s 8(1); place of supply (s 13) asked as a fact
    `I am registered or required to be registered`                IS A BOOLEAN   -- s 8(2): a "taxable person"
    `it is in the course or furtherance of my business`           IS A BOOLEAN   -- s 8(1); "business" (s 3) asked as a fact
    `it is done for a consideration`                              IS A BOOLEAN   -- s 10(2)(a): something given in return
    `the Second Schedule treats it as a supply`                   IS A BOOLEAN   -- s 10(1): e.g. a deemed supply; asked as a fact
    `what I supply`                                               IS A `What I supply`
    `it is goods that are exported`                               IS A BOOLEAN   -- s 21(1)
    `it is international services`                                IS A BOOLEAN   -- s 21(1), (3); asked as a fact

-- The four answers the Act gives about a supply. "Outside the scope" is this encoding's gloss for a supply on which s 8(1) charges
-- nothing because one of its conditions fails.
DECLARE `How GST treats my supply` IS ONE OF
    `outside the scope of GST`   -- no tax: s 8(1) is not met, or it is not a "supply" (s 10(2)(a))
    `exempt`                     -- s 22(1): no tax, and not a taxable supply
    `zero-rated`                 -- s 21(2): taxable supply at a nil rate
    `standard-rated`             -- s 8(1), s 16: tax at the rate in force

GIVEN `my supply` IS A `My supply`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is it a supply at all` IF
        `my supply`'s `it is done for a consideration`
    OR  `my supply`'s `the Second Schedule treats it as a supply`

-- READING (inference): where a supply is both of a Fourth Schedule description and exported or international services, s 21(2)'s
-- "whether or not tax would be chargeable on the supply apart from this section" is read as making it zero-rated. First Schedule
-- para 8(1)(a)(ii) still calls such financial services "exempt supplies"; that tension is recorded in NOTES.md.
GIVEN `my supply` IS A `My supply`
GIVETH A `How GST treats my supply`
`How is my supply treated` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT `Is it a supply at all` `my supply`                                    THEN `outside the scope of GST`
        IF NOT `my supply`'s `it is made in Singapore`                                THEN `outside the scope of GST`
        IF NOT `my supply`'s `I am registered or required to be registered`           THEN `outside the scope of GST`
        IF NOT `my supply`'s `it is in the course or furtherance of my business`      THEN `outside the scope of GST`
        IF `my supply`'s `it is goods that are exported` OR `my supply`'s `it is international services` THEN `zero-rated`
        IF `Is what I supply exempt` (`my supply`'s `what I supply`)                  THEN `exempt`
        OTHERWISE                                                                          `standard-rated`

GIVEN `my supply` IS A `My supply`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must I charge GST on my supply` IF
    `How is my supply treated` `my supply` EQUALS `standard-rated`

-- s 8(2A)(a), with s 21(2)(b): a zero-rated supply is "in all other respects treated as a taxable supply". A taxable supply is what
-- counts towards registration (First Schedule para 1) and what input tax may be attributed to (s 20(2)(a)).
GIVEN `my supply` IS A `My supply`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is my supply a taxable supply` IF
        `How is my supply treated` `my supply` EQUALS `standard-rated`
    OR  `How is my supply treated` `my supply` EQUALS `zero-rated`

§§ `11(2) -- when my supply takes place`

-- s 11(2): a supply "is treated ... as taking place at the time when -- (a) the person making the supply issues an invoice or
-- receives any consideration in respect of it; or (b) where both events occur, the first of the 2 events occurs, to the extent that
-- the supply is covered by the invoice or consideration".
-- The partial-coverage split ("to the extent") is not encoded: one invoice or one payment is taken to cover the whole supply. Where
-- neither has happened yet, s 11(2) does not fix a time, and the answer is NOTHING. The s 11(3) cases (land, an approved
-- application, agents) are not encoded.
GIVEN `the day I issue the invoice` IS A MAYBE DATE
      `the day I receive payment`   IS A MAYBE DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`When does my supply take place` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the day I issue the invoice`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN `the day I receive payment`
    WHEN JUST `invoice day` THEN
        CONSIDER `the day I receive payment`
        WHEN NOTHING THEN JUST `invoice day`
        WHEN JUST `payment day` THEN
            IF `payment day` LESS THAN `invoice day`
            THEN JUST `payment day`
            ELSE JUST `invoice day`

§§ `16 -- the rate`

-- s 16: "Tax is charged at the rate of -- (a) 4% for the period from 1 January 2003 to 31 December 2003 (both dates inclusive);
-- (b) 5% for the period from 1 January 2004 to 30 June 2007 ...; (c) 7% for the period from 1 July 2007 to 31 December 2022 ...;
-- (ca) 8% for the period from 1 January 2023 to 31 December 2023 ...; and (cb) 9% from and including 1 January 2024".
-- The section as deposited states no rate before 1 January 2003. For an earlier day this encoding returns NOTHING: the Act in this
-- edition does not say, and earlier rates are not guessed.
GIVEN `the day my supply takes place` IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`What is the GST rate in percent on the day my supply takes place` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the day my supply takes place` AT LEAST Date 1 1 2024 THEN JUST 9
        IF `the day my supply takes place` AT LEAST Date 1 1 2023 THEN JUST 8
        IF `the day my supply takes place` AT LEAST Date 1 7 2007 THEN JUST 7
        IF `the day my supply takes place` AT LEAST Date 1 1 2004 THEN JUST 5
        IF `the day my supply takes place` AT LEAST Date 1 1 2003 THEN JUST 4
        OTHERWISE NOTHING

§§ `17(2) -- how much of my price is GST`

-- s 17(2): "If the supply is for a consideration in money, its value is taken to be such amount as, with the addition of the tax
-- chargeable, is equal to the consideration." The price I agree already contains the tax: at 9%, $109 is a value of $100 and $9 of
-- GST. Not for a reverse charge supply or a redeliverer's supply (s 17(2A)), which are not encoded.
GIVEN `the price I charge` IS A NUMBER, `the rate in percent` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`What is the value of my supply` MEANS
    `the price I charge` TIMES 100 DIVIDED BY (100 PLUS `the rate in percent`)

GIVEN `the price I charge` IS A NUMBER, `the rate in percent` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`How much of my price is GST` MEANS
    `the price I charge` MINUS `What is the value of my supply` `the price I charge` `the rate in percent`

§§ `40 -- a contract made before the rate changed`

-- s 40(1): where, after the contract and before the supply, "there is a change in the tax charged on the supply, then, unless express
-- provision for the exclusion of any such change in the tax charged is contained in the contract or where the change in the tax has
-- been taken into account", (a) for an increase "the supplier MAY ADD to the agreed price in the contract the amount of ... the
-- increase in that tax"; (b) for a reduction "the supplier MAY DEDUCT from the agreed price ... the reduction of that tax".
-- Both limbs are permissions. So the most I may charge after an increase is the price with the increase added; after a reduction,
-- (b) permits but does not require a deduction, and the most I may charge stays the agreed price. (Reading; see NOTES.md.)
-- The agreed price is taken to contain GST at the old rate (s 17(2)).
GIVEN `the agreed price`                          IS A NUMBER
      `the rate in percent when I contracted`     IS A NUMBER
      `the rate in percent when I supply`         IS A NUMBER
      `the contract expressly excludes the change` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the change was taken into account`         IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A NUMBER
`What is the most I may charge under a contract made before the rate changed` MEANS
    IF     `the contract expressly excludes the change`
        OR `the change was taken into account`
        OR `the rate in percent when I supply` AT MOST `the rate in percent when I contracted`
    THEN `the agreed price`
    ELSE `the agreed price` TIMES (100 PLUS `the rate in percent when I supply`)
                            DIVIDED BY (100 PLUS `the rate in percent when I contracted`)

§§ `19 -- input tax and what I pay for a period`

-- s 19(3)(a): "input tax" is tax on supplies to me (and on my imports and reverse charge supplies), "being ... goods or services used
-- or to be used for the purpose of any business carried on ... by the taxable person".
-- s 19(4): where they are used "partly for the purposes of a business ... and partly for other purposes, tax ... must be apportioned
-- so that only so much as is referable to the taxable person's business purposes is counted as the taxable person's input tax".
GIVEN `the GST I paid on a purchase` IS A NUMBER, `the business share in percent` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`How much of the GST I paid counts as my input tax` MEANS
    `the GST I paid on a purchase` TIMES `the business share in percent` DIVIDED BY 100

-- s 19(2): at the end of each prescribed accounting period I am "entitled ... to credit for so much of [my] input tax as is allowable
-- under section 20, and then to deduct that amount from any output tax that is due". s 19(5): if "the amount of the credit exceeds that
-- of the tax, then ... the amount of the excess must be paid to the taxable person by the Comptroller" -- subject to s 19(5A), (6),
-- (7), which are not encoded. How much input tax s 20 and the regulations allow is an input.
DECLARE `What happens for the period` IS ONE OF
    `I must pay the Comptroller`  HAS `the amount to pay` IS A NUMBER     -- s 19(1), (2)
    `the Comptroller must pay me` HAS `the amount to get back` IS A NUMBER -- s 19(5)
    `nothing is due either way`

GIVEN `my output tax for the period`            IS A NUMBER
      `my allowable input tax for the period`   IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A `What happens for the period`
`What do I pay or get back for the period` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `my output tax for the period` GREATER THAN `my allowable input tax for the period`
            THEN `I must pay the Comptroller` (`my output tax for the period` MINUS `my allowable input tax for the period`)
        IF `my allowable input tax for the period` GREATER THAN `my output tax for the period`
            THEN `the Comptroller must pay me` (`my allowable input tax for the period` MINUS `my output tax for the period`)
        OTHERWISE `nothing is due either way`
